<TopicPage slug="subjects/biology/intermediate/neurobiology-basics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Neurobiology studies the <strong>cells and circuits behind behaviour</strong> — the
    biological layer between chemistry and psychology. Its ground truth: behaviour is
    electrochemical. Signals travel as spikes (the neuroscience subject dissects one), chemicals
    carry them across synapses, and circuits of repeated use get stronger. Brain, meet hardware.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four principles that organise nervous systems" widget="equationStep" config={neurobioCfg} />

  <h2>Scaling up to brains</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Numbers:</strong> ~86 billion neurons, ~100 trillion synapses in a human brain — wired not randomly but by genetic and activity-driven rules.</li>
    <li><strong>Specialisation with integration:</strong> visual cortex for sight, hippocampus for memory, cerebellum for movement timing — but no brain region works alone; consciousness itself (still debated) presumably emerges from the integration.</li>
    <li><strong>Evolution built in layers:</strong> brainstem (breathing, reflexes — ancient and shared with reptiles) under limbic system (emotion, memory) under cortex (planning, language). The layers mostly cooperate; addiction and anxiety are what it looks like when older layers outvote newer ones.</li>
    <li><strong>The neuroscience ladder</strong> goes deeper: anatomy, action potentials, senses, memory circuits, and the consciousness debate.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Kandel et al., Principles of Neural Science; Hebb (1949); Azevedo et al., J. Comp. Neurol. (2009) on neuron counts.</p>
</TopicPage>
